menu "espix usb"

    config ESPIX_USB_VERBOSE
        bool "Verbose USB host logging"
        depends on ESPIX_USB_ROLE_HOST
        default n
        help
            Raise the USB stack's own log tags to DEBUG, so `dmesg` shows what
            the host library and the class drivers think is happening: port
            changes, what was enumerated and at what speed, and why a device was
            not claimed by any driver.

            This is the switch to reach for when a device does not appear. The
            stack logs everything useful at DEBUG and almost nothing at INFO, so
            with this off a working enumeration and an empty socket look
            identical -- which is exactly the question it exists to answer.

            Two halves, and both are needed. CONFIG_LOG_MAXIMUM_LEVEL (DEBUG, in
            sdkconfig.defaults) decides whether the messages exist in the image
            at all; this option raises the tags that are noisy enough to be worth
            leaving off by default.

            Costs nothing when off, and when on it costs log volume: the kernel
            log ring holds 96 lines (ESPIX_KLOG_LINES) and this will fill it.
            Nothing else in the system is raised -- WiFi, lwip and the app loader
            keep their own levels.

    config ESPIX_USB_VERIFY_READS
        int "Verify this many block reads after boot"
        depends on ESPIX_USB_ROLE_HOST
        default 0
        range 0 100000
        help
            Verify the first N block reads after boot: each is read a second
            time and compared, and a read whose verification disagrees is
            re-read until two consecutive reads agree (or failed after four
            tries). 0 disables it.

            This exists because a device can return wrong data and report success,
            and nothing above the block device can tell. The filesystems do notice
            -- FatFs reports FR_INT_ERR from exFAT's entry-set checksum, lwext4
            logs a failed directory checksum -- but neither says whether the
            medium lied or the read failed, and a listing that stopped early looks
            exactly like a short directory.

            A majority vote is deliberately NOT used: a device that returns the
            same wrong bytes to every read wins the vote and the lie is returned
            as success. Two reads that agree is the strongest claim available
            without a checksum on the medium.

            It costs a second read of every verified transfer, so it is bounded
            and off by default: on a 12 Mbps link that is expensive, and a mount's
            first accesses are where the damage was measured (the first 200). Set
            it to cover that, reboot, read `dmesg`, and turn it off again. See
            docs/KNOWN-ISSUES.md.

endmenu